WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.400
I had known about this story

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.920
for decades.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.240
The disaster of 1871

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.760
was one of the the things 
that basically ended Yankee

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.160
whaling in the in the early 20th century,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.840
because it was such a devastating loss.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.760
What was still there,
if anything, was still

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.200
there, was a question
that needed to be answered.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.960
I was the mission coordinator.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.480
Once we got there, it was deeply personal.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.360
We were going out we were 
going to essentially develop

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.640
a map of the seabed between Point

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.760
Franklin and Wainwright.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.560
I remember sitting on the after
deck of the vessel

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.960
we were on and thinking to myself,
you know,

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.640
this is the same place
where the whalers tucked in.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.960
They were here and now we're here.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.320
And and that was a very
powerful experience for me.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.760
One of the first things
we did was to scan using sonar,

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.520
with sonar

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.840
you're basically bouncing
sound waves off the seabed

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.680
and building a picture
from the returning signal.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.400
What you're looking for are straight
lines, which are rare in nature.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.400
What's it look like on the screen?

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.480
Suddenly you see these straight lines

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.440
and then more horizontal figures

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.480
blocks and like that.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.680
That looks interesting.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.040
That could be a piece of a shipwreck.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.920
The sonar picked up six features
that appeared to be manmade.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.720
So the sonar can be a bit misleading.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.360
Is it a provocatively 
shaped group of rocks

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.800
or is that the outline of a hull?

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.520
That's when you need to put eyes on.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.800
Diving was not an option.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.760
You're literally in water
that's just above freezing.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.120
You get into that water and, it hurts.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.320
To avoid diving,

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.680
we created a drop camera system
that can be lowered to the seabed.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.640
We started to drag the drop camera

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.120
around, trying to get a better view of
what was there.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.040
See that? What is that?

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.160
Suddenly this structure appeared.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.440
It was a mass of heavy wood.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.480
You could see sections of the hull

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:01.200
that were there indicating
some of that structure had survived.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.800
You had the broken ends
of the ribs of the frames

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.560
that have been gnawed by the ice
but is covered in marine growth.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.760
You had some stone ballast as well

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.440
that helped stabilize that ship
when it was afloat.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.960
You go, okay,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.160
we've got a shipwreck.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.080
Pieces of copper
sheathing were visible on sections

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.680
of planking next to the 70 foot long hull.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.600
It was clear evidence
that what we were looking at

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.840
was a vessel that at least been built
in the 19th century.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.480
We now knew a shipwreck
could survive underwater from the North

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:38.400
Alaskan coast.

